# Chapter 1: Introduction

Before writing a single SQL query, you should first explore and understand the database you're working with.

One of the most common mistakes beginners make is jumping straight into writing queries without understanding the relationships between tables, the available columns, or the purpose of the data. Spending a few minutes exploring the database can save hours of debugging later.

Remember:

> **Think before you code.**

A piece of advice from one of my professors has stayed with me throughout my studies:

> **"If you understand first, think second, and write third, you'll rarely have to rewrite your code."**

This principle applies not only to SQL but to programming, data analysis, and software engineering in general.

In this chapter, we will learn how to:

- Explore the available databases
- Inspect tables and their columns
- Understand primary keys and relationships
- Identify the purpose of each table
- Build a mental model of the database before writing queries

A good SQL developer doesn't just write queries—they understand the data first.
